# Shell scripts must keep LF endings even when checked out on Windows.
#
# With Git's default `core.autocrlf=true` on Windows, docker-entrypoint.sh is
# rewritten to CRLF at checkout. COPY-ing it into a Linux image then leaves the
# shebang reading `#!/bin/sh\r`, so the kernel looks for an interpreter whose
# name ends in a carriage return and the container exits immediately with
# `exec /usr/local/bin/docker-entrypoint.sh: no such file or directory` —
# a message that points at the script rather than at the interpreter.
*.sh text eol=lf

# YAML is LF-standard; CI/workflow contract tests parse these files and
# Windows checkouts otherwise rewrite them to CRLF.
*.yml text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf

# Source, docs, and prompt templates are compared byte-exactly by golden
# tests (e.g. lib/pbl/v2 prompt snapshots): with core.autocrlf=true a
# Windows checkout rewrites them to CRLF and the goldens fail with
# identical-looking diffs whose only difference is an invisible carriage
# return. Pin them to LF so every platform checks out the same bytes.
*.ts text eol=lf
*.tsx text eol=lf
*.mts text eol=lf
*.mjs text eol=lf
*.md text eol=lf
